  {
   "id": "prerun",
   "when": "13:51",
   "title": "Work from before the run, by an agent that had seen the answer texts, could be read",
   "happened": "A folder the proving agents were allowed to read still held earlier attempts and proofs written before the run by a Claude agent that built the harness and had read the texts of the true statements. One Claude proving agent used that material for 2 of its statements. Also readable: the first answer key’s statement list, whose lists identify 41 of its 44 planted texts (this run uses the same texts, renumbered), and one accepted proof from before the run.",
   "done": "At 13:51 UTC all of it (11 folders, the first statement list and the accepted proof) was moved out of the folders the agents may read; a note stands in its place.",
   "scoring": "Those 2 statements are marked contaminated for the Claude family, which is reported with and without them. The blinding audit lists every read of the moved material by any agent of either family, with its time; any statement decided after such a read is reported separately."
  },
  {
   "id": "decoy-text",
   "when": "13:29",
   "title": "A correct refutation would have stopped the run",
   "happened": "A refutation proves that a statement’s text, taken alone without its “may use” hypotheses, is false. A decoy whose text is a planted (false) text is true as a whole, because one of its hypotheses is false, yet its text can be refuted. The protocol said that refuting a decoy meant a kernel or judge fault and stopped the run. This was found during the run and written down before the answer key is opened.",
   "done": "Ruled at 13:29 UTC, before the key is opened: such a refutation is correct. It is reported in its own column, “text refuted”, beside “proved”, and it does not stop the run. A family that refuted such a decoy first can no longer prove it, and the report says so.",
   "scoring": "The rule that stops the run stays for a refutation of a true original or of a decoy whose text is true."
  },
  {
   "id": "vacuous",
   "when": "13:47",
   "title": "A planted statement can be true, vacuously",
   "happened": "A Claude agent found that a planted statement’s “may use” list can name a decoy whose text is false. The planted statement then holds vacuously and has a correct kernel proof, and the gate “zero planted statements proved” would have flagged a correct proof as a fault.",
   "done": "Ruled at 13:47 UTC, before the key is opened: the gate judges what the kernel judged. Zero “proved” on a statement whose goal is false, and zero “refuted” on a statement whose text is true; either one would mean a kernel or judge fault. A planted statement whose goal holds vacuously is scored in its own class.",
   "scoring": "Every statement is classed by the truth of what the kernel was asked to judge, read from the key once it is opened."
  },
  {
   "id": "protocol",
   "when": "14:06",
   "title": "A sentence in the protocol named one statement and both families’ outcomes on it",
   "happened": "The text of the 13:29 ruling, added to the protocol file the agents may read, named one statement and how both families had decided it. Both had already decided it, so no attempt on it could change; the sentence could still hint at the role of the statements that share its text.",
   "done": "At 14:06 UTC the statement and its outcomes were taken out of the protocol file; the case is kept in the private work log.",
   "scoring": "The blinding audit counted the reads of the protocol after 13:29 that showed the sentence: 2 by Claude agents, 1 by a GPT session."
  },
  {
   "id": "audits",
   "when": "15:29",
   "title": "What the two blinding audits found",
   "happened": "After the run, a Claude agent read every agent’s tool log (906 Claude tool calls, 973 GPT shell commands). All nine GPT sessions were clean; six of the nine Claude agents were not. It found two reads the agents had not reported: two Claude agents read the whole proof from before the run in the run’s first minutes, and a third read the texts of the first statement list. Nobody read the answer key or the other family’s folder, or ran HOL4 outside the judge.",
   "done": "A GPT-6.2 agent then audited the same logs on its own (at 16:08 UTC). It agreed on every agent and every read that matters, and differed on the size of three sets (the table below). Scoring uses the union of the two, the stricter reading.",
